WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:04.937
This is a robot cell: an industrial robot, bolted to the floor,

2
00:00:05.088 --> 00:00:06.412
with a fence around it.

3
00:00:07.457 --> 00:00:12.740
On the left a belt brings parts up to a stop, so each part lies in a known place.

4
00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:17.492
The robot puts them on the loading table of the machine on the right.

5
00:00:18.594 --> 00:00:21.706
Outside the fence stands the controller, with the drives.

6
00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:26.154
Each joint has a servo motor that drives to an exact position.

7
00:00:27.168 --> 00:00:30.256
So it repeats the same cycle, day in, day out.

8
00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:33.730
Each turning joint is an axis.

9
00:00:34.423 --> 00:00:37.778
The base turns the arm round, shoulder and elbow lift,

10
00:00:37.987 --> 00:00:41.133
and three axes in the wrist turn and tilt the hand.

11
00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:43.658
On the wrist sits a gripper:

12
00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:46.885
two fingers that close round the part on compressed air.

13
00:00:47.572 --> 00:00:49.081
The tool decides the job.

14
00:00:50.087 --> 00:00:54.325
The program is a list of positions of one point: the tool centre point,

15
00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.564
the working tip of the tool, here between the fingers.

16
00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:03.173
If the tool gets bent in a crash, every position is off by the same amount.

17
00:01:03.947 --> 00:01:07.093
After a crash, the setter checks the tool centre point.

18
00:01:08.457 --> 00:01:12.486
You teach positions with the teach pendant: a heavy tablet on a cable,

19
00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.924
with a joystick and an emergency stop.

20
00:01:15.992 --> 00:01:19.556
On the back sits the enabling switch, with three positions.

21
00:01:20.237 --> 00:01:22.547
The robot only moves in the middle one.

22
00:01:23.325 --> 00:01:26.785
Let go, or squeeze hard in a fright, and it stops.

23
00:01:27.785 --> 00:01:32.533
In manual mode the tool moves at most 250 millimetres a second.

24
00:01:33.282 --> 00:01:37.531
A point 1.5 metres away takes at least 6 seconds.

25
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.295
With the cell empty, it runs in automatic at full speed.

26
00:01:43.021 --> 00:01:46.214
Teaching can take hours; a cycle then takes seconds.

27
00:01:47.551 --> 00:01:51.289
At the end of the wrist sits the flange: a round mounting plate.

28
00:01:52.362 --> 00:01:56.135
What is bolted on is called end-of-arm tooling: here a gripper.

29
00:01:57.159 --> 00:02:01.826
The payload is what the robot may carry at its flange, including the gripper.

30
00:02:02.572 --> 00:02:06.531
A 4 kilo gripper plus a 6 kilo casting is 10 kilos.

31
00:02:07.587 --> 00:02:10.768
On a 10 kilo robot that is 100 percent,

32
00:02:10.977 --> 00:02:14.774
and the weight sits 150 millimetres off the flange.

33
00:02:15.478 --> 00:02:17.881
The payload diagram then allows less.

34
00:02:19.262 --> 00:02:23.256
This articulated robot has six axes, like a human arm,

35
00:02:23.442 --> 00:02:26.461
and usually reaches 0.7 to 3 metres.

36
00:02:27.623 --> 00:02:32.267
A SCARA robot has four axes and swings very fast in a horizontal plane:

37
00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.380
picking and placing small parts,

38
00:02:34.566 --> 00:02:35.832
or driving screws.

39
00:02:36.847 --> 00:02:41.178
A cobot is built to work next to people: it limits force and speed.

40
00:02:41.904 --> 00:02:46.432
But a cobot with a knife is not safe: it is the application that counts.

41
00:02:47.456 --> 00:02:51.310
Industrial robots work behind a fence, a light curtain,

42
00:02:51.461 --> 00:02:55.699
or an area scanner that slows the robot when someone walks into a zone.

43
00:02:56.406 --> 00:02:58.473
Every cell gets a risk assessment.

44
00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.592
Usually the machine sets the pace: the robot waits for the door.

45
00:03:04.315 --> 00:03:09.110
The question is how long the machine stands still while the robot swaps parts.

46
00:03:10.104 --> 00:03:12.147
The lathe runs for 40 seconds.

47
00:03:12.878 --> 00:03:17.626
With a single gripper it stands still for 20 seconds: 60 seconds in all,

48
00:03:17.754 --> 00:03:19.228
60 parts an hour.

49
00:03:20.249 --> 00:03:25.230
A double gripper arrives with the raw part already in hand and swaps in one visit:

50
00:03:25.427 --> 00:03:26.577
8 seconds stopped,

51
00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:28.028
75 an hour.

52
00:03:28.727 --> 00:03:29.934
Do check the payload.

53
00:03:30.981 --> 00:03:33.164
Feeding is often harder than the robot.

54
00:03:33.943 --> 00:03:37.844
A vibratory bowl feeder shakes small parts up a spiral track,

55
00:03:38.006 --> 00:03:40.177
so they come out in one orientation.

56
00:03:41.505 --> 00:03:45.139
The open front has a light curtain: a row of light beams.

57
00:03:45.862 --> 00:03:48.649
When a hand breaks a beam, the robot stops.

58
00:03:49.637 --> 00:03:54.235
But not at once: the stopping time runs from beam broken to robot still.

59
00:03:54.952 --> 00:03:57.855
Measure it on the real robot, with the real gripper.

60
00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.834
Meanwhile the hand moves on.

61
00:04:01.545 --> 00:04:06.375
So the curtain hangs at a safety distance: S equals K times T plus C,

62
00:04:06.468 --> 00:04:09.069
with K two thousand millimetres a second.

63
00:04:10.098 --> 00:04:15.392
A curtain with a 30 millimetre resolution gives a C of 128.

64
00:04:16.158 --> 00:04:24.297
At 0.15 seconds, S is 300 plus 128: 428 millimetres.

65
00:04:25.181 --> 00:04:28.467
Above 500, you recalculate with a lower K.

66
00:04:29.755 --> 00:04:33.575
With vision, a camera finds parts that are not in a fixed place,

67
00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.536
and tells the robot where to pick.

68
00:04:36.602 --> 00:04:40.828
In bin picking, a 3D camera looks into a box of loose parts.

69
00:04:41.532 --> 00:04:45.247
Ask for the pick rate on your own parts, the last ones too.

70
00:04:46.255 --> 00:04:47.613
In offline programming,

71
00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:52.501
you test the program in a 3D model on a PC while the cell keeps producing.

72
00:04:53.203 --> 00:04:55.351
On site you touch up a few points.

73
00:04:56.664 --> 00:05:00.496
A cobot without a fence often works with power and force limiting:

74
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.773
contact is allowed,

75
00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.700
below limits per body region.

76
00:05:04.402 --> 00:05:05.900
That usually means slow.

77
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.989
Say the takt is 14 seconds, and the risk assessment allows only half speed.

78
00:05:12.722 --> 00:05:17.343
The 6 second move becomes 12, plus 4 for the rest: 16 seconds.

79
00:05:18.075 --> 00:05:18.679
Too slow.

80
00:05:19.656 --> 00:05:24.741
Speed and separation monitoring also works on an industrial robot like this one:

81
00:05:24.916 --> 00:05:26.448
when someone comes closer,

82
00:05:26.622 --> 00:05:28.131
a scanner slows it down.

83
00:05:29.148 --> 00:05:34.465
With nobody near, it runs at full speed: 10 seconds, inside the takt.

84
00:05:35.228 --> 00:05:38.641
So ask how often someone really walks into the zone.
